Design of `check_predicate_consistency.py`:

```python
#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Proposed gate rule: visibility-predicate consistency for WCC vacuum classes.
Standalone; no repo imports. Exits 0 if verdict != fail (pass/not_applicable), 1 if fail.
"""
CLI: schema_path, --json OUT, --class-id (optional expected), --label
```

Implementation details:
- `strict_load(text)`: compose; duplicate path list; safe_load.
- Detect applicability: `d.get('visibility')` is dict and `visibility.get('predicate_name')` and (`visibility.get('definition')` or `visibility.get('role')=='conclusion'`). If no `visibility.definition` and no predicate_name → not_applicable.
- Canonical predicate checks:
  - `pred_name = visibility.predicate_name`; `vis_def`.
  - `pred_called = pred_name in conclusion.statement_formal`.
  - tail markers in vis_def: `tail` (case-insens), `t0 in [0,T)`, `gamma([t0,T))`.
  - assertive whole/union reading in vis_def: regexes:
    - union reading: `union of J\^-\(q\)` or `J\^-\(I\+\)` or `gamma\(\[0,T\)\)\s*is contained in the union`
    - whole-curve single-q: `iff\s+gamma\(\[0,T\)\)\s+is contained in\s+J\^-\(q\)` (as the defining clause)
    Only a fail if tail markers are absent OR the sentence carrying the whole/union reading is not repudiated. Simpler and safer: if the *defining clause* after `iff` contains a whole-curve/union reading, fail. Implement by extracting the sentence containing "iff" (up to first ". ") and checking it for `[0,T)` containment patterns without `[t0,T)`. In M1: `iff gamma([0,T)) is contained in the union of J^-(q) over all q in I+ intersected with M.` → contains `[0,T)` but not `[t0,T)` → fail. In rev12: `iff there exists q in I+ AND t0 in [0,T) such that the TAIL gamma([t0,T)) ...` → contains `[t0,T)` → pass.
- R3 formal: extract `quantifiers.formal`; tail markers present; `pred_name` present OR expansion with `[t0,T)`; fail if assertive whole clause regex `not exists q in I\+ with gamma subset` / `gamma\(\[0,T\)\)\s*(subset|is contained)` present (M0: `not exists q in I+ with gamma subset B.` → pattern `with gamma subset` catches; also `B` marker). We can check: contains `gamma subset` (whole-curve shorthand, no t0) → fail. And require `(q,t0)` or `t0 in [0,T)` or `gamma([t0,T))`.
- R4 D5: find the domain id bound by the `not_exists` binders whose binder mentions `q` — or simply `quantifiers.ordered` entry with `kind == not_exists` → domain_id. Then d5 = domains[domain_id].definition. Check: tail markers present (`tail` and `gamma([t0,T))` and `t0`); fail if the definition contains an assertive whole-curve clause: regex `gamma\(\[0,T\)\)\s+is contained in the causal past` and no repudiation markers (`NOT`, `stronger`, `strictly`) — but M2's replaced line will be `pairs defined by whole-curve containment: gamma([0,T)) is contained in the causal past J^-(q) intersected with M` → tail markers absent too, so fail on "missing tail markers". Rev12 D5 has tail markers + repudiation sentence "Whole-curve containment ... is strictly STRONGER and is NOT the predicate". To avoid false positive on the repudiation, require tail markers AND (no whole-curve assertive clause OR repudiation present). Implement `assertive_whole_curve(text)` returning matched sentences minus those containing repudiation words (`not`, `stronger`, `must not`, `never`). Careful: the sentence "Whole-curve containment gamma([0,T)) subset J^-(q) is strictly STRONGER and is NOT the predicate of this class" contains "NOT" → repudiated. Good.
- R5 negation: `quantifiers.negation`: require tail markers (`tail` and `visible from I+`); fail if the negation text contains assertive whole-curve/B clause: regex `gamma\(\[0,T\)\)\s*(subset|is contained).*J\^-\(q\)` without repudiation, or `contained in B`/`inside B` as the definition of failure. M4: `has no q in I+ with gamma subset J^-(q) (whole-curve B reading)` → regex `gamma subset J\^-\(q\)` → fail. Also check `visibility.negation_conclusion` requires `every t0` tail form (rev12: "for every q in I+ and every t0 in [0,T), the tail ... is NOT contained") — fail if it lacks `t0`. This is a useful extra.
- R6 variant-not-assertive: scan assertive surface (`quantifiers.formal`, `quantifiers.negation`, `conclusion.statement_formal`, `visibility.definition`) for variant SET markers: `union of J\^-\(q\) over all q` / `J\^-\(I\+\)` / `set-based visibility`. Fail if present without repudiation. M1 → fail.
- R7 optional `class_id_matches` when `--class-id` given: fail if schema class_id != expected (catches wrong-file binding). This is class-bound evidence.
- Each rule yields {rule_id, ok, detail, evidence_lines}.
- Verdict: fail if any required rule fails; not_applicable if no visibility predicate; pass otherwise.
- Output JSON: {"rule": "W061-PREDICATE-CONSISTENCY-V1", "target": path, "sha256":..., "class_id":..., "applicable":..., "verdict":..., "failed_rules":[...], "rules":[...], "generated_at":...}
- Exit code: 0 pass/NA, 1 fail, 2 usage/parse error. Hmm: for a gate, parse error is a fail (exit 1) with failed rule R0. But gate runner should distinguish. I'll use exit 1 for fail including parse failure, exit 0 otherwise; `--strict-exit` not needed.

Line evidence: helper `find_lines(text, needle)` returns line numbers.

